  run_ids: ["43934d8f-bf75-474c-b935-52f825b5c9ef"] -> ["b1129318-0ca0-421f-8d16-a9068880d039"]
  title: "Exact integer and floating bit serialization, with explicit failure conditions" -> "Exact endian writes and selected prefix reads, with failure contracts"
  explanation: "byteorder 1.5.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. The two handwritten Default methods always panic." -> "byteorder 1.5.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. BE/LE read_u24 additionally returns the exact unsigned24 prefix decode below2^24, and asserts on shorter input. LittleEndian from_slice_u16/u32/u64/u128 additionally leave every slice unchanged on the verified little-endian target, including empty slices and arbitrary unsigned values. The two handwritten Default methods always panic. Four unsigned variable-width readers decode the exact selected prefix under their complete byte-count/input-length guard, and assert otherwise."
  trusted_assumptions: "F32/F64 are explicit 32/64-bit logical patterns; two executable std to_bits models preserve those patterns as unsigned words, with checked Lean value-preservation lemmas. Guards require original writer bodies and allow no other float operation. Kani checks std bit identity for every pattern and actual released writers with 6/10-byte buffers. Connecting these Rust checks to the Lean representation is reviewed, not mechanized; Rust nightlies differ. Official translation/standard integer and slice models, Lean, and Kani/CBMC are trusted. Mapped audits permit only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound." -> "F32/F64 are explicit 32/64-bit logical patterns; two executable std to_bits models preserve those patterns as unsigned words, with checked Lean value-preservation lemmas. Guards require original writer bodies and allow no other float operation. Kani checks std bit identity for every pattern and actual released writers with 6/10-byte buffers. Connecting these Rust checks to the Lean representation is reviewed, not mechanized; Rust nightlies differ. Official translation/standard integer and slice models, Lean, and Kani/CBMC are trusted. The four original native conversion bodies make no external calls after official target-branch translation. Five unused declarations remain in the unchanged, unimported template; the provider supplies zero definitions. Guards require exact identity bodies and zero external use. Mapped audits permit only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound."
  evidence_url: "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/c730051590dce08c1ede987497d6e45efb2f839a" -> "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/92ec1a332f271ae96a414c880caf1ec5d3673b4c"
  limitations: "This covers 20 selected writer methods and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Reads, remaining signed variable-width wrappers, bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified." -> "This covers 20 selected writer methods, six unsigned readers, four native-target unsigned identity conversions, and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Fixed 16/32/64/128-bit reads, signed readers, variable-width writers, other bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. The signed from_slice defaults contain unsafe slice reinterpretation and are not covered; the floating conversions and opposite-endian/other-target paths are also unproved. The byteorder-lite eight-method result is not assumed transferable across versions. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified."
